{"qid":"Q195972","names":["Kingdom of Kartli"],"title":"Kingdom of Kartli","endonym":null,"capsule":"Kartli (known to Greeks as Iberia) was the core Georgian kingdom centred on Mtskheta and, from the fifth century, Tbilisi, occupying the Kura basin in the eastern Caucasus. At its apex under Vakhtang I Gorgasali (r. c. 447–502) it controlled much of the South Caucasus and sought Byzantine alliance against the Sasanians. Caught between Ottoman and Safavid expansion, the kingdom was partitioned by the Peace of Amasya (1555) and definitively absorbed into the Safavid provincial system under Shah Abbas I after 1603, though a client Georgian dynasty continued until Russian annexation in 1801.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Territorial extent is moderately attested through Georgian chronicles and Safavid-Ottoman treaty texts; boundaries shifted significantly across the 1463–1706 period and are coarsely reconstructed by modern historians.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Encyclopaedia Iranica: Kartli","url":"https://www.iranicaonline.org/articles/kartli"},{"label":"Britannica: Kartli","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Kartli"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Kingdom of Kartli","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Kingdom_of_Kartli"}],"britannica":null,"cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Kingdom of Kartli","rulers":[{"qid":null,"name":"Bagrat VI","nativeName":"ბაგრატ VI","roman":"","start":1466,"end":1478,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name":"Constantine II","nativeName":"კონსტანტინე II","roman":"","start":1478,"end":1505,"image":null,"imageUrl":null,"imageLicense":null},{"qid":null,"name